To calculate the value of a fraction with the denominator being the square root of the quotient of a and b, while ensuring that the numerator and denominator are both prime numbers, you can use the following C++ code:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
bool isPrime(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) {
        return false;
    }
    for (int i = 2; i <= sqrt(n); i++) {
        if (n % i == 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
int main() {
    int a, b;
    cout << "Enter the values of a and b: ";
    cin >> a >> b;
    int denominator = sqrt(a / b);
    int numerator = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i <= sqrt(denominator); i++) {
        if (denominator % i == 0 && isPrime(i)) {
            numerator *= i;
            denominator /= i;
            i--;
        }
    }
    if (isPrime(denominator)) {
        numerator *= denominator;
        denominator = 1;
    }
    cout << "The fraction is: " << numerator << "/" << denominator << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
This code first takes the values of a and b as input from the user. It then calculates the denominator as the square root of the quotient of a and b. Next, it finds the prime factors of the denominator and multiplies them to get the numerator. It ensures that the numerator and denominator are both prime numbers. Finally, it outputs the fraction.
To ensure that this fraction results in the most optimal value for c/d, we can simplify the fraction by dividing both the numerator and denominator by their greatest common divisor. We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor of the numerator and denominator.
The Latex equation format for this calculation is:
$$\frac{p}{q} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\frac{a}{b}}}$$
where p is the numerator and q is the denominator.

