Here is an example of using the 'strings' package in Go to search for the word 'error' within a log file:
```
package main
import (
	"fmt"
	"io/ioutil"
	"strings"
)
func main() {
	content, err := ioutil.ReadFile("logfile.txt")
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println(err)
	}
	lines := strings.Split(string(content), "\n")
	for _, line := range lines {
		if strings.Contains(line, "error") {
			fmt.Println(line)
		}
	}
}
```
One potential challenge of using this method is that it may not be as memory efficient as other methods for large files, since the entire file content is read into memory. To address this, we can read the file content in chunks instead of all at once. Another challenge is that it may not be as flexible as 'grep' command in terms of search options. This can be addressed by implementing additional search options in the code.

