Is there a way to calculate the total cost of items in a dynamically generated list without using loops in Java? The input list will have items in the following format:
List<String> items = new ArrayList<String>();
items.add("Item Name : Price");
I want to extract the price value and calculate the total cost without using any loops in Java. Can this be achieved through a more efficient method?

output:
Yes, it is possible to calculate the total cost of items without using loops in Java. One way to achieve this is by using the stream() method of the ArrayList class and chaining it with other stream operations. Here's an example code snippet to achieve this:
```
List<String> items = new ArrayList<String>();
items.add("Item 1 : 10");
items.add("Item 2 : 20");
items.add("Item 3 : 30");
double totalCost = items.stream()
                .map(item -> item.split(" : ")[1])
                .mapToDouble(Double::parseDouble)
                .sum();
System.out.println("Total cost: " + totalCost);
```
In this code, the stream() method is used to create a stream of the items in the list. The map() method is used to extract the price value from each item string by splitting it using the delimiter " : " and selecting the second element of the resulting array. The mapToDouble() method is then used to convert each price string to a double value, and finally, the sum() method is used to calculate the total cost of all items.

