What JavaScript data structure would you recommend to efficiently track the frequency of each word in a document, considering its size and complexity? Here's a sample JavaScript code that uses an object to accomplish this task:
const document = "This is a sample document containing sample words.";
const words = document.split(' ');
const wordFreq = {};
words.forEach(word => {
  if (word in wordFreq) {
    wordFreq[word]++;
  } else {
    wordFreq[word] = 1;
  }
});
//Print out the frequency of each word
Object.keys(wordFreq).forEach(key => {
  console.log(key + ": " + wordFreq[key]);
});
How would you modify this code to handle case-insensitive word frequency tracking?

output:
To efficiently track the frequency of each word in a document, considering its size and complexity, I would recommend using a JavaScript Map data structure. Here's how you can modify the code to handle case-insensitive word frequency tracking using a Map:
```
const document = "This is a sample document containing sample words.";
const words = document.toLowerCase().split(' ');
const wordFreq = new Map();
words.forEach(word => {
  if (wordFreq.has(word)) {
    wordFreq.set(word, wordFreq.get(word) + 1);
  } else {
    wordFreq.set(word, 1);
  }
});
//Print out the frequency of each word
wordFreq.forEach((value, key) => {
  console.log(key + ": " + value);
});
```
In this modified code, we first convert all the words to lowercase using the `toLowerCase()` method to handle case-insensitive word frequency tracking. Then, we use the `Map()` constructor to create a new Map data structure. We use the `has()` method to check if the word is already in the map and the `set()` method to add the word and its frequency to the map. Finally, we use the `forEach()` method to iterate over the map and print out the frequency of each word.

Here is a JavaScript code solution to generate a secure password that meets the criteria you specified:
```
function generatePassword() {
  const uppercaseLetters = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ";
  const lowercaseLetters = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz";
  const numbers = "0123456789";
  const specialCharacters = "!@#$%^&*()_+-={}[]|;:,.<>?";
  const allCharacters = uppercaseLetters + lowercaseLetters + numbers + specialCharacters;
  const forbiddenCharacters = "password0123456789";
  let password = "";
  while (password.length < 8) {
    const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * allCharacters.length);
    const randomCharacter = allCharacters.charAt(randomIndex);
    if (password.length === 0 && forbiddenCharacters.includes(randomCharacter)) {
      continue;
    }
    if (password.length > 0 && password.includes(randomCharacter)) {
      continue;
    }
    password += randomCharacter;
  }
  return password;
}
console.log(generatePassword());
```
This code defines four strings that contain all the uppercase letters, lowercase letters, numbers, and special characters that can be used in the password. It also defines two additional strings: `allCharacters` that contains all the characters that can be used in the password, and `forbiddenCharacters` that contains all the characters that cannot be used in the password.
The code then initializes an empty string `password` and enters a while loop that continues until the `password` string has a length of 8 characters. In each iteration of the loop, the code generates a random index into the `allCharacters` string and selects the character at that index. It then checks if the character is forbidden (if it is the first character and is in the `forbiddenCharacters` string) or if it has already been used in the password. If either of these conditions is true, the code skips to the next iteration of the loop. Otherwise, the character is added to the `password` string.
Finally, the code returns the generated password.

