To extract the color information from the sentence "The sky was an incredible shade of blue," you can use regular expression matching in Go to look for color keywords like "blue." Then, you can use a color library in Go to convert the color name to its corresponding RGB, hex code, and HSL values. Finally, you can format the color information into a JSON object using the built-in encoding/json package in Go.
Here's an example Go code snippet that accomplishes this:
```
package main
import (
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
	"regexp"
	"github.com/gookit/color"
)
type Color struct {
	RGB  string `json:"rgb"`
	Hex  string `json:"hex"`
	HSL  string `json:"hsl"`
	Name string `json:"name"`
}
func main() {
	sentence := "The sky was an incredible shade of blue"
	// Define a list of color keywords to match against
	colorKeywords := []string{"blue", "sky"}
	// Compile a regular expression pattern to match color keywords
	pattern := regexp.MustCompile(fmt.Sprintf(`\b(?:%s)\b`, regexp.QuoteMeta(color.Join(colorKeywords, "|"))))
	// Find the first color keyword in the sentence
	match := pattern.FindStringSubmatch(sentence)
	if len(match) == 0 {
		fmt.Println("No color found in sentence")
		return
	}
	colorName := match[0]
	// Convert the color name to RGB, hex code, and HSL values
	rgb := color.NameToRGB(colorName)
	hex := rgb.ToHexString()
	hsl := rgb.ToHSL().String()
	// Format the color information into a JSON object
	colorObj := Color{
		RGB:  rgb.String(),
		Hex:  hex,
		HSL:  hsl,
		Name: colorName,
	}
	colorJSON, err := json.Marshal(colorObj)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Error marshaling JSON:", err)
		return
	}
	fmt.Println(string(colorJSON))
}
```
This code uses the `gookit/color` library to convert color names to RGB values. If you don't want to use this library, you can use a different color library or create your own mapping of color names to RGB values.

