Yes, it is possible to create a C++ program that can identify the sentence type, tense, and mood. To accomplish this, you would need to use natural language processing (NLP) techniques and tools.
One popular NLP tool is the Stanford Parser, which is a natural language parser that can analyze the grammatical structure of a sentence and identify the sentence type, tense, and mood. To use the Stanford Parser in C++, you would need to download the parser and include its libraries in your code.
Here is an example code that uses the Stanford Parser to analyze a sentence and identify its type, tense, and mood:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include "stanford-parser/stanford_parser.h"
using namespace std;
int main() {
    // Initialize the Stanford Parser
    StanfordParser parser("stanford-parser/englishPCFG.ser.gz");
    // Input a sentence
    string sentence = "I am eating a sandwich.";
    // Parse the sentence
    ParseTree tree = parser.parse(sentence);
    // Get the sentence type
    string type = tree.getSentenceType();
    // Get the tense and mood
    string tense = tree.getTense();
    string mood = tree.getMood();
    // Print the results
    cout << "Sentence type: " << type << endl;
    cout << "Tense: " << tense << endl;
    cout << "Mood: " << mood << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we first initialize the Stanford Parser by loading the English PCFG grammar. We then input a sentence and parse it using the `parse` function. We can then use the `getSentenceType`, `getTense`, and `getMood` functions to extract the type, tense, and mood of the sentence, respectively. Finally, we print the results to the console.
Note that you would need to include the Stanford Parser libraries and make sure they are properly linked in your code. Additionally, you would need to download the English PCFG grammar file and specify its location in the `StanfordParser` constructor.

